  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal temperatures and mainly dry weather \nwill continue through the upcoming week, with a few showers possible \nacross the Southern Tier at times.\n\nAn upper level trough and closed low will slowly move east across \nthe Midwest to Ohio Valley through mid-week. High pressure will \nremain across eastern Canada, bringing continued northeast flow \nacross the eastern Great Lakes region though a majority of the work \nweek. \n\nA shortwave trough will move across the Ohio Valley this evening. \nSubsidence and dry air will maintain mostly dry weather across the \nregion, however isentropic lift and deep moisture will approach the \nNY/PA line. Widely scattered showers are possible across the western \nSouthern Tier through tonight, mainly near the PA state line. \nOtherwise, a dense shield of cirrus will continue across the eastern \nGreat Lakes through much of the night, with some partial clearing \npossible towards daybreak. Low temperatures will fall to the 40s \nwith locations across the North Country possibly seeing the upper \n30s. Confidence in temperatures reaching the upper 30s is low, as \nthe position of the high well to the north and uncertainty in cloud \ncover does not offer the best set-up for radiational cooling. With \nthat being said, there is a non-zero chance of patchy frost in known \ncold spots across Jefferson and Lewis counties tonight.\n\nLow-level easterly flow will increase to our south ahead of the \napproaching closed low Tuesday. A few showers along the NY/PA border \ncan't be ruled out. An upper level ridge is expected to move south \nfrom eastern Canada for the end of the work week, and widespread dry \nweather is anticipated across the forecast area. Temperatures will \ngradually increase each day but stay below normal for late \nSeptember.",
